Q3. What is the difference between super and this keyword?
Ans. * 'this' is a reserved keyword and provides a way to refer current object within a class's methods or a constructor.
     * this() call invokes another constructor within the same class, which must be the first line of the constructor.

It can be used in various contexts as given below:

to refer instance variable of current class
to invoke or initiate current class constructor
can be passed as an argument in the method call
can be passed as argument in the constructor call
can be used to return the current class instance

* 'super' is a reserved keyword and allows access to the members(methods, constructor) of the immediate parent class.
* super() invokes the constructor of the parent class.

super can be used in various contexts as given below:

it can be used to refer immediate parent class instance variable
it can be used to refer immediate parent class method
it can be used to refer immediate parent class constructor.

They both are non-static keywords and can be used anywhere except static area.

Q4. Difference between instance variable and local variable?
Ans. The distinction between instance variables and local variables lies primarily in their scope, lifetime, and declaration location.

Instance Variables:

* Declaration: Declared within a class, but outside of any method, constructor, or block.
* Scope: Accessible by all methods within the class. They belong to a specific object (instance) of the class.
* Lifetime: Exist as long as the object they belong to exists in memory. Each object has its own copy of the instance variables.
* Initialization: If not explicitly initialized, they are automatically assigned default values (e.g., 0 for numeric types, 
null for object references, false for booleans). 

Local Variables:
* Declaration: Declared inside a method, constructor, or a specific block of code. 
* Scope: Limited to the method, constructor, or block in which they are declared. They are not accessible outside this defined scope. 
* Lifetime: Created when their containing method or block is entered and destroyed when the method or block exits.
* Initialization: Must be explicitly initialized before use. 
In Java, the compiler will generate an error if a local variable is used without being initialized, but in C++, it doesn't give compile
error, but give a giberish value.

Q5. Can we override a private or static method?
Ans. No, it is not possible to override private or static methods in Java.

Private Methods: They are inaccessible outside the class in which they are declared, hence even subclasses won't be able to override them.
If a subclass declares a method with the same name and signature as a private method in its superclass, 
it is considered a new, independent method within the subclass, not an overridden method.

Static Methods: Method overriding is a concept of dynamic polymorphism, which relies on dynamic binding at runtime. As static methods are 
associated with class and not with instance(object) of class, They are bound at compile time (static binding).
If a subclass declares a static method with the same name and signature as a static method in its superclass, 
this is known as method hiding, not overriding. In this case, the method invoked depends on the declared type of the reference variable, 
not the actual object type.

