Q9. What are friend functions and friend classes?
Ans. * Friend Functions: A friend function is a special function that is allowed to access private and protected data of a class, 
even though it's not a member of the class.
Example:

#include <iostream>

class MyClass {
private:
    int privateData;

public:
    MyClass(int data) : privateData(data) {}

    // Declare a friend function
    friend void displayPrivateData(const MyClass& obj);
};

// Define the friend function outside the class
void displayPrivateData(const MyClass& obj) {
    std::cout << "Private data: " << obj.privateData << std::endl;
}

int main() {
    MyClass obj(10);
    displayPrivateData(obj); // Call the friend function
    return 0;
}

While friend function is is declared within the class using the friend keyword, it is not considered a member of that class 
and is not invoked using the member-selection operators (. or ->)

* Friend Class: A friend class is a class that can access the private and protected members of another class.
Example:

class MyClass {
private:
    int privateData;

public:
    MyClass(int data) : privateData(data) {}

    // Declare FriendClass as a friend
    friend class FriendClass; 
};

class FriendClass {
public:
    void accessMyClassData(MyClass& obj) {
        // FriendClass can access privateData of MyClass
        obj.privateData = 100; 
        std::cout << "Accessed privateData: " << obj.privateData << std::endl;
    }
};

int main() {
    MyClass obj(50);
    FriendClass friendObj;
    friendObj.accessMyClassData(obj); 
    return 0;
}

Q10. What are virtual function and pure virtual function?
Ans. * Virtual Function: A virtual function is a member function that is declared within a base class using the keyword virtual 
and is re-defined (Overridden) in the derived class. Virtual functions enable runtime polymorphism (late binding), calling the correct function 
via a base class pointer or reference.

Have a look at example from here: https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/cpp/virtual-function-cpp/

* Pure Virtual Function: A pure virtual function is a function in a base class with = 0 and no body, which must be overridden in derived 
classes. A class with such a function is called abstract and cannot be instantiated.

Have a look at example here: https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/cpp/virtual-function-cpp/

class Base
{
  public:
  
    // Pure virtual function
    virtual void display() = 0;

    // Pure virtual destructor
    virtual ~Base() = 0;
};

